============================================================
BUDGET BURN MODEL
============================================================

Generate a simple LINEAR BUDGET BURNDOWN chart.

The X axis is calendar date.

The Y axis is Remaining Budget.

Create two lines:

    1. Ideal Remaining Budget
    2. Actual Remaining Budget

IDEAL REMAINING BUDGET
----------------------

On the Start Date:

    Ideal Remaining Budget = Full Budget

On the End Date:

    Ideal Remaining Budget = 0

Between those dates, decrease the ideal remaining budget linearly according to
elapsed calendar time.

Conceptually:

    ideal_remaining =
        budget * (1 - elapsed_days / total_plan_days)

The implementation must correctly handle inclusive Start/End dates and avoid
divide-by-zero errors.

ACTUAL REMAINING BUDGET
-----------------------

Actual remaining budget is:

    Budget - cumulative qualifying spending

where qualifying spending means:

    purchase date is inside the Budget Plan interval

AND

either:

    Category Scope = ALL

or:

    Category Scope = SELECTED
    and purchase broad Category is one of the selected Categories.

Actual spending must accumulate chronologically.

The actual line should therefore show how much budget would remain after the
qualifying purchases known as of each date.

If spending exceeds the budget, Actual Remaining Budget may go below zero.

Do NOT clamp it to zero because overspending needs to remain visible.

============================================================
CHART INTERPRETATION
============================================================

The chart should make this relationship intuitive:

    Actual Remaining > Ideal Remaining
        spending is running below the planned burn rate

    Actual Remaining < Ideal Remaining
        spending is running above the planned burn rate

    Actual Remaining approximately equals Ideal Remaining
        spending is approximately on budget pace

